  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how different microscopic excitations in a quantum material can be selectively driven and what kinds of emergent effects may follow. On the left, four main degrees of freedom are illustrated: electronic charge, electronic spin, orbital character, and lattice vibrations (phonons). Each is shown with a simple cartoon of its excitation process, suggesting that external stimulation can target charge transitions, spin rotation or ordering, orbital rearrangement, or atomic motion in the lattice.\n\nThese ingredients are then connected to a central crystal-like quantum material, depicted as an ordered array of atoms. A red waveform directed at the material represents an external time-dependent drive, likely light or another electromagnetic excitation, acting on the solid. The figure’s overall message is that driving specific internal modes of a material can reshape its collective behavior.\n\nOn the right, the possible outcomes are summarized as several classes of phenomena: driving a Higgs mode, driving lattice motion, changing band topology, inducing phase transitions, and creating or suppressing quasiparticles. Taken together, the image presents a broad conceptual map of nonequilibrium control in quantum materials, where manipulating charge, spin, orbital, or phonon excitations leads to tunable macroscopic states and dynamics.",
